From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: d2d1: layers, primitive blends and DrawImage's composite modes

PushLayer/PopLayer were stubs: what a program drew inside a layer went
straight to the target, opaque and unclipped. A layer is now a bitmap of the
target's size that drawing goes to until PopLayer, which puts it on the
target through the content bounds, the geometric mask (drawn in the world at
push time, the bitmap as a brush), the opacity and the opacity brush. Layers
nest; EndDraw pops any left; the D2D1_LAYER_PARAMETERS1 PushLayer and
INITIALIZE_FROM_BACKGROUND are honoured.

SetPrimitiveBlend was recorded but everything was drawn source-over, and
DrawImage ignored its composite mode. Each mode is now a D3D blend state
(premultiplied: a source and a destination factor), made on first use:
COPY, ADD, MIN, MAX, and all thirteen composite modes; the next drawing is
source-over again.

Paint.NET 5 draws parts of its UI through layers and composite modes.

Gate: test/d2dlayer-gate.sh (make test-d2dlayer; 19 checks, stock fails 12).
